# Apex — Invocable Action Structure

## Required pattern

```java
public class MyAction {

    public class Request {
        @InvocableVariable(label='Record Id' required=true)
        public String recordId;
    }

    public class Response {
        @InvocableVariable(label='Status')
        public String status;
    }

    @InvocableMethod(
        label='My Action Label'
        description='What this action does.'
        callout=true   // required if ANY code path makes an HTTP callout
    )
    public static List<Response> run(List<Request> requests) {
        List<Response> responses = new List<Response>();
        for (Request req : requests) {
            responses.add(process(req));
        }
        return responses;
    }

    private static Response process(Request req) {
        Response resp = new Response();
        try {
            // ... logic here ...
            resp.status = 'OK';
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            System.debug('MyAction error: ' + ex.getMessage() + '\n' + ex.getStackTraceString());
            resp.status = 'Error: ' + ex.getMessage();
        }
        return resp;
    }
}
```

## Key rules

- `@InvocableVariable` is required on every field in Request and Response — without it, Flow cannot bind the field
- `callout=true` on `@InvocableMethod` is required even if only one code path makes a callout — omitting it causes a runtime error the first time a callout is attempted
- Always return a `List<Response>` — Flow passes requests in bulk even when only one record triggers
- Never throw from the `@InvocableMethod` method — catch all exceptions, write to `System.debug`, and return a status string so Flow execution can continue
- Do all DML in a single consolidated write at the end of `process()` — multiple DML statements on the same record within one transaction cause lock errors

## Passing lists of values from Flow to Apex

Flow cannot pass `List<String>` directly as an `@InvocableVariable`. Use comma-separated strings and split/filter in Apex:

```java
// In Request
@InvocableVariable(label='Product Codes (comma-separated)') public String productCodes;

// In Apex
List<String> codes = new List<String>();
for (String s : req.productCodes.split(',')) {
    if (!String.isBlank(s)) codes.add(s.trim());
}
```
